# Every kind of disc this program can be asked to make.
#
# Ten axes. Multiplied out they are 5 x 3 x 4 x 4 x 6 x 6 x 2 x 2 x 2 x 2,
# which is 138,240 projects, and nobody is testing that. What matters is that
# every PAIR of values appears somewhere, because failures come of one thing
# or two things interacting far more often than of six.
#
# PICT turns this into the shortest set of rows that covers every pair, and
# tests\DiscWright.Tests\EveryKindOfDisc.cs runs every row of it. Regenerate
# with tests\by-hand\Build-TheCombinations.ps1 after changing anything here.
#
# THE CONSTRAINTS ARE NOT TIDINESS. Each one is a rule the program really has,
# and without it PICT invents projects that cannot exist and the test fails on
# cases that are nobody's bug.

Kind:        GogInstaller, GogWithAddOns, FilesWithOneToRun, FilesWithSeveralToRun, FilesWithNothingToRun
Games:       1, 2, 3
Biggest:     small, mostOfACd, biggerThanACd, overTwoGiB
Size:        CD, DVD5, DVD9, BD25
Artwork:     none, icon, background, cover, discFace, allOfIt
Extras:      none, manual, extrasFolder, loose, music, allOfIt
Menu:        on, off
Checksums:   on, off
Legacy:      on, off
Split:       on, off

# Add-ons come out of what was left beside a GOG installer. A folder of game
# files leaves nothing behind, because that recogniser takes everything in it,
# so those three kinds can never have any.
# written as a constraint PICT rightly said it was always true, so it is a
# comment: GogWithAddOns is a value of Kind rather than an axis of its own.

# ISO9660 cannot describe a file over 2 GiB, and the program refuses rather
# than finding out after copying 7 GB. That is its own case in the suite; here
# it would make every row with a big file about one thing.
IF [Biggest] = "overTwoGiB" THEN [Legacy] = "off";

# A tune only reaches the disc when there is a menu to play it, so pairing
# music with no menu says nothing that the menu-off cases do not already say.
IF [Extras] = "music" THEN [Menu] = "on";

# A file bigger than the disc with nothing to cut it is a refusal, and a
# refusal is one state however it was arrived at. Half the rows would be that
# one sentence. The cases that matter are covered deliberately in the suite.
IF [Biggest] = "overTwoGiB" THEN [Size] <> "CD";
